  <!-- ========== DELTA ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="delta">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      What Moved (Last 24-48h)
      <span class="meta">Descriptive · banned-verb lint enforced · each paragraph cites a forecast or is marked context-only</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="delta-narrative">
      <p>The National (6&#160;July) reported the UK-France multinational mine-countermeasure coalition — comprising HMS Dragon, RFA Lyme Bay as command vessel, French MCM ships and frigates, and contingents from Germany, the Netherlands, Belgium, Norway, Italy, Canada, and Denmark — is ready to begin Hormuz clearing operations within days. France confirmed on 4&#160;July that two MCM vessels, two frigates, and a maritime patrol aircraft are deployed to the Hormuz area. NATO Secretary-General announced in Brussels that the mission fills a critical operational gap. US MCM ships USS Pioneer (MCM-9) and USS Chief (MCM-14), repositioned on 11&#160;April, have remained active in the strait throughout the period. → moved <span class="cite">F5</span></p>
      <p>The state funeral for Ali Khamenei — who died on 28&#160;February&#160;2026 — continued 5–7&#160;July with large public gatherings in Tehran, Qom, and Mashhad. Mojtaba Khamenei, installed as Supreme Leader under IRGC pressure on 9&#160;March, was absent from the burial ceremony in Mashhad on 6&#160;July. Iranian state media published official memorial imagery featuring Mojtaba but carried no live address, verified video, or directly attributed statement from him during the four-day period. Time (5&#160;July) reported his continued absence from public view; Iran International confirmed reports of facial and leg injuries from the 28&#160;February attack. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>
      <p>Qatar confirmed on 2&#160;July that no high-level US-Iran negotiating session would be scheduled until after the funeral period ends (approximately 9&#160;July). VP Vance told reporters on 1&#160;July that talks are "going well" and that nuclear file discussions are expected to begin. Iranian negotiators in Doha discussed frozen asset release procedures under the Islamabad MOU framework at the last session (1–2&#160;July), but the Hormuz transit-toll dispute — Iran's demand for a per-vessel fee, US rejection of any transit tax — remained unresolved. A US-IRGC military exchange on 28&#160;June (CENTCOM struck ten Iranian targets; IRGC responded with strikes on Bahrain and Kuwait) was followed by a Qatar-brokered ceasefire reaffirmation on 29&#160;June; no further confirmed bilateral exchange has occurred since. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>
      <p>Knesset Legal Adviser Sagit Afik recommended 17&#160;July as the dissolution date and October&#160;27 as the fixed election date. Knesset Speaker Amir Ohana and Coalition Chair Ofir Katz both confirmed the October&#160;27 timeline in coordinated statements reported by Times of Israel (30&#160;June). The two preliminary dissolution readings passed 106–0 and 110–0 in cross-party votes that included the opposition. Netanyahu stated publicly on 30&#160;June that he is seeking a "broad coalition" for the election campaign, his first explicit acknowledgment that the election itself — rather than the coalition's continued survival — is the operative variable. → supports <span class="cite">F7</span></p>
      <p>IMF PortWatch data through early July recorded the 7-day rolling average at approximately 32–36 commercial transits per day in the Strait of Hormuz. War-risk insurance rates at Lloyd's and the P&amp;I market remained elevated; no reinstatement of cover for unescorted Hormuz transit has been confirmed by any major insurer. The Islamabad MOU Article&#160;4 sets a demining completion deadline of approximately 17&#160;July. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

